<section class="card">
  <h2 style="margin-top:0">Commenting on DUE</h2>
  <p>DUE is now comments-only for the public. Drafting and edits happen in the backend, but readers can share feedback on published essays once comments open.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Where to comment:</strong> visit any published essay and scroll to the comments section.</li>
    <li><strong>What to include:</strong> note whether your feedback is <em>minor</em> (typos, phrasing, references) or <em>major</em> (structural or thematic changes). The form lets you pick either intent before you type.</li>
    <li><strong>Stay concise:</strong> keep topics and keywords tight (≤5 each) so discussions stay focused.</li>
    <li><strong>Be respectful:</strong> follow the project’s tone and cite sources when you can.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h2>What happens to your feedback</h2>
  <p>Comments are pre-moderated. Accepted notes may be published under the essay with a status such as “Not yet implemented” or “Implemented.”</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Minor feedback is acknowledged in the essay credits once incorporated.</li>
    <li>Major feedback that materially shifts the essay is tracked in release notes and version bumps.</li>
    <li>For sensitive issues, flag them in your comment so moderators can review before posting.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h2>Code of conduct for commenters</h2>
  <p>Keep discussion productive and evidence-based. Comments that include harassment, spam, or unrelated self-promotion will be rejected.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Stay respectful: critique ideas, not people, and avoid personal attacks or discrimination.</li>
    <li>Be clear and cite sources when challenging facts or recommending changes.</li>
    <li>Protect privacy: do not post personal data about yourself or others.</li>
    <li>Keep it relevant: stay on the essay’s topic and declare conflicts of interest.</li>
    <li>One account per person: do not create multiple personas to influence moderation.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h2>Privacy statement</h2>
  <p>When you submit a comment, we store the text, the name or handle you provide, and any optional contact details to support moderation, follow-up, and version tracking.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Data use: your comment and chosen display name may be published under the essay once approved. Optional contact info is used only for clarifications.</li>
    <li>Retention: comments and their moderation history stay on record so version histories remain auditable.</li>
    <li>Removal and corrections: request updates or deletion by contacting the maintainers through the About page.</li>
    <li>Cookies and analytics: no advertising trackers are used; only essential site cookies are required for basic functionality.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h2>Terms of use</h2>
  <p>By commenting, you grant DUE permission to publish, edit for clarity, and archive your contribution alongside the relevant essay.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Licensing: you retain ownership of your words while allowing DUE to display and moderate them.</li>
    <li>Moderation rights: comments may be declined, edited for tone or brevity, or removed if they break this policy.</li>
    <li>No professional advice: essays and comments are informational only and not medical, legal, or financial advice.</li>
    <li>Attribution: credit for accepted changes appears in release notes or acknowledgments based on impact.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h2>Semantic version rules for essays</h2>
  <p>Each essay uses a three-part version number (for example, <code>version 1.2.6</code>) to show how much has changed.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Right number:</strong> minor fixes such as typo corrections or adding a reference.</li>
    <li><strong>Middle number:</strong> major edits like adding several sentences or a whole paragraph.</li>
    <li><strong>Left number:</strong> tracks completion and meaning shifts—<code>0</code> for unfinished, <code>1</code> for finished, and higher if the conclusion or core message changes.</li>
    <li><strong>Starting points:</strong> essays published before they are finished are labeled “Unfinished on time” and begin at <code>version 0.1.0</code>; essays published when or after they are finished are labeled “Finished on time” and begin at <code>version 1.0.0</code>.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h2>While comments are closed on drafts</h2>
  <p>Drafts remain visible so readers can see what is coming next, but comments open only after publication. Check back on the publish date listed in the draft schedule.</p>
</section>

